# CLI Command Patterns

## Adding a New Command

1. Create `internal/cli/<command>.go`
2. Define `var <command>Cmd = &cobra.Command{...}` with `Use`, `Short`, `Long`, `RunE`
3. Register flags in `init()` — use `StringP`, `BoolP` for shorthand flags
4. Register the command in `root.go` `init()`: `rootCmd.AddCommand(<command>Cmd)`

## Command Structure

```go
var exampleCmd = &cobra.Command{
    Use:   "example",
    Short: "One-line summary",
    Long:  `Multi-line description with examples...`,
    RunE:  runExample,
}

func init() {
    exampleCmd.Flags().StringP("flag", "f", "default", "Description")
}

func runExample(cmd *cobra.Command, args []string) error {
    setupVerbose(cmd)
    // ...
}
```

## Shared Helpers

- `setupVerbose(cmd)` — call at the start of every `RunE` function
- `createMachineFromFlags(cmd, text)` — creates Enigma from CLI flags
- `createMachineFromPreset(name)` — creates from named preset
- `createMachineFromConfig(path)` — loads from JSON config file
- `writeOutput(text, cmd)` — handles stdout vs file output
- `preprocessInput(cmd, text)` — applies --remove-spaces, --uppercase, etc.

Do NOT duplicate filter/transform functions across commands — reuse from `encrypt.go`.

## Output Convention

- Use `cmd.OutOrStdout()` for normal output, `cmd.ErrOrStderr()` for diagnostics
- Verbose messages go to stderr so stdout stays clean for piping
